/Contents (Note 34\072 Matters of significant concern to the public\056 These are matters sufficiently important to the public\054 rather than every trivial or purely personal subject\056 The following sentence gives examples\056 The rule does not require the subject to fall within that politician\220s individual portfolio\056 The Bill\220s other requirements still determine whether any offence has been committed\056)
/Dest [ 122 0 R /FitR 299.6378 506.9898 557.2756 740.8898 ]
/Rect [ 323.7609 197.1298 546.0894 211.2898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 35\072 \215Such matters include\216\056 \215Include\216 introduces examples\054 not a closed list\056 Public health or public safety\054 for example\054 may qualify even though not separately named\056 Any other subject must still meet the requirement of significant public concern\056 The judicial and solely private exclusions continue to apply\056)
/Dest [ 122 0 R /FitR 38 248.5898 295.6378 435.8898 ]
/Rect [ 46 180.3298 155.876 194.4898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 36\072 Public policy\056 Plans\054 decisions and proposals about what government or public bodies should do\056 This can include policies being proposed\054 opposed or debated\054 as well as policies already in force\056 The amendment\220s honest\055opinion protection leaves room to argue about which policies are best\056)
/Dest [ 122 0 R /FitR 299.6378 248.5898 557.2756 435.8898 ]
/Rect [ 158.7223 180.3298 224.9878 194.4898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 37\072 National security\056 Matters affecting the country\220s security\054 such as defence or threats to the state\056 Listing the subject does not require disclosure of protected secrets\056 The Bill\220s existing defences remain\054 including the specified intelligence or active\055service defence and the reasonable\055excuse defence\056)
/Dest [ 130 0 R /FitR 38 537.3898 295.6378 740.8898 ]
/Rect [ 231.0203 180.3298 318.0914 194.4898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
214 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 38\072 Public expenditure\056 Spending public money\072 for example\054 budgets\054 the cost of a policy or what a public programme will receive\056 A disagreement about spending priorities can be an honest political opinion\056 A dishonest factual claim about a figure is a different matter\073 the offence\220s full requirements still apply\056)
/Dest [ 130 0 R /FitR 299.6378 553.5898 557.2756 740.8898 ]
/Rect [ 324.1239 180.3298 421.9867 194.4898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
215 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 39\072 Allocation of public assets or resources\056 Decisions about who receives or uses things held for public purposes\056 Examples include public land\054 buildings\054 equipment\054 funding and staff time\056 \215Allocation\216 means how those assets or resources are assigned or distributed\056 This reaches more than money alone\056)
/Dest [ 130 0 R /FitR 38 233.0898 295.6378 435.8898 ]
/Rect [ 428.0192 180.3298 546.9469 194.4898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
216 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 39\072 Allocation of public assets or resources\056 Decisions about who receives or uses things held for public purposes\056 Examples include public land\054 buildings\054 equipment\054 funding and staff time\056 \215Allocation\216 means how those assets or resources are assigned or distributed\056 This reaches more than money alone\056)
/Dest [ 130 0 R /FitR 38 233.0898 295.6378 435.8898 ]
/Rect [ 46 163.5298 143.6899 177.6898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
217 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 40\072 Legislative or democratic processes\056 The making and changing of laws\054 and processes through which democratic decisions are made\054 such as elections and referendums\056 This describes the subject being discussed\056 It does not add referendum campaign staff to the people covered\054 and it does not remove paragraph \05015\051\220s protection for parliamentary proceedings\056)
/Dest [ 130 0 R /FitR 299.6378 233.6898 557.2756 435.8898 ]
/Rect [ 149.7225 163.5298 329.7646 177.6898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
218 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 41\072 The environment\056 Matters such as pollution\054 climate\054 nature\054 air\054 water and land\056 Naming the environment makes clear that important environmental issues are within the examples of public concern\056 As elsewhere\054 honest disagreement and genuine mistakes remain protected\054 and the offence\220s other conditions must be satisfied\056)
/Dest [ 137 0 R /FitR 38 538.6898 295.6378 740.8898 ]
/Rect [ 358.337 163.5298 445.1258 177.6898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 42\072 Section 12\0504\051\072 the journalism exception\056 Clause 12\0504\051 otherwise excludes acts done for journalism by a recognised news publisher or someone working for one\056 Paragraph \05016\051 removes that exception for the listed person\220s qualifying public communication\056 A politician therefore cannot rely on that exception for such a communication simply because it forms part of a journalistic role\056)
/Dest [ 137 0 R /FitR 299.6378 506.9898 557.2756 740.8898 ]
/Rect [ 451.1583 163.5298 521.3245 177.6898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 43\072 \215That person\216 and \215such a communication\216\056 These words tie the removal of the journalism exception to the particular listed legislator\054 minister or mayor and a communication meeting paragraph \05016\051\220s conditions\056 They do not remove the exception generally for the publisher or other journalists\056 The politician\220s remaining offence safeguards and defences still apply\056)
/Dest [ 137 0 R /FitR 38 218.1898 295.6378 435.8898 ]
/Rect [ 110.9807 146.7298 357.6514 160.8898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 44\072 \215This sub\055paragraph\216\056 This means \05016\051 alone\056 The final sentence switches off its special public\055communications rule for communications made in a judicial capacity and communications concerning solely private affairs\056 It does not create a blanket exemption from section 12 or from other laws\056)
/Dest [ 137 0 R /FitR 299.6378 248.5898 557.2756 435.8898 ]
/Rect [ 363.6839 146.7298 466.0293 160.8898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 45\072 Made in a judicial capacity\056 This means acting as a judge\054 for example when delivering a judgment\056 A serving judge can remain a member of the House of Lords while barred from sitting and voting there\056 These words prevent the new communications rule from applying to judicial work simply because the judge is also a peer\056)
/Dest [ 145 0 R /FitR 38 537.3898 295.6378 740.8898 ]
/Rect [ 149.1175 129.9298 286.9781 144.0898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
223 0 obj
<<
/Border [ 0 0 0 ]
/Contents (Note 46\072 Solely private\054 family or business life\056 This keeps purely private matters outside the new communications rule\056 \215Solely\216 means entirely\072 public money\054 public resources or official decisions can make something more than a private affair\054 even where a family business is involved\056 The sentence limits this rule\073 it does not create a general exemption from the Bill\056)
/Dest [ 145 0 R /FitR 299.6378 538.6898 557.2756 740.8898 ]
/Rect [ 303.8715 129.9298 547.0794 144.0898 ]
/Subtype /Link
/Type /Annot
>>
endobj
